  <!-- SEO CONTENT -->

  <section id="learn">
    <div class="container">

      <div class="seo-content">

        <h2>What Is Trading Backtesting?</h2>

        <p>
          Trading backtesting is the process of testing a trading strategy
          against historical market data to understand how the strategy
          could have performed under previous market conditions.
        </p>

        <p>
          A proper backtesting process can help traders study entries,
          exits, stop loss placement, take profit levels, risk management,
          win rate, drawdown and overall strategy performance.
        </p>


        <h2>Why Use a Trading Backtesting Platform?</h2>

        <p>
          Manually scrolling through historical charts can make it easy
          to accidentally see future price movements. A market replay
          system can hide future candles and allow traders to make
          decisions sequentially.
        </p>

        <p>
          BFT Trade Lab is being developed around this idea: practice
          historical trading conditions, record decisions and analyze
          the results systematically.
        </p>


        <h2>XAUUSD and Gold Trading Backtesting</h2>

        <p>
          Gold traders can use historical XAUUSD price action to study
          setups such as breakouts, fake breakouts, support and resistance,
          retests, trend continuation and liquidity-based trading concepts.
        </p>


        <h2>Trading Strategy Testing</h2>

        <p>
          A strategy should not be judged only by a few profitable trades.
          Traders can evaluate a larger sample of historical trades using
          metrics such as win rate, average reward-to-risk ratio,
          profit factor and maximum drawdown.
        </p>


        <h2>Backtesting vs Forward Testing</h2>

        <p>
          Backtesting uses historical market data. Forward testing,
          often performed through paper trading or simulated trading,
          evaluates how a strategy behaves on new market data that was
          not used during the original strategy development.
        </p>


        <h2>Trading Risk Management</h2>

        <p>
          Risk management is an important part of strategy testing.
          Position sizing, stop loss, account risk and risk-to-reward
          calculations can be analyzed alongside trading performance.
        </p>


        <h2>Learn Trading Through Practice</h2>

        <p>
          BFT Trade Lab is designed as a practice and analysis environment,
          not as a promise of trading profits. The goal is to help traders
          develop a systematic process for testing, recording and
          evaluating trading decisions.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- FAQ -->

  <section>
    <div class="container">

      <div class="section-title">
        <h2>Trading Backtesting FAQ</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="faq">

        <details>
          <summary>What is trading backtesting?</summary>
          <p>
            Trading backtesting means testing a trading strategy using
            historical market data before applying the strategy to
            future markets.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What is market replay?</summary>
          <p>
            Market replay recreates historical price movement step by
            step so traders can make decisions without seeing future
            candles in advance.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What is forward testing?</summary>
          <p>
            Forward testing evaluates a strategy on new market data,
            usually using simulated or paper trades rather than real
            money.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I backtest a gold trading strategy?</summary>
          <p>
            BFT Trade Lab is being designed to support historical
            XAUUSD and other market data for strategy testing.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can backtesting guarantee trading profits?</summary>
          <p>
            No. Historical performance does not guarantee future results.
            Backtesting should be used for research, practice and
            strategy evaluation.
          </p>
        </details>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

